'''annominate''' (a-nom′i-nāt), ''v. t.'' [Another form of ''agnominate'', q. v.] To name; especially, give a punning or alliterative name to. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|How then shall these chapters be ''annominated?''
{{right|''Southey'', Doctor, viii. § 1.}}}}

'''annomination''' (a-nom-i-nā′shọn), ''n.'' [< L. ''annominatio(n-)'', ''adnominatio(n-)'', for *''adgnominatio(n-)'', usually ''agnominatio(n-)'': see ''agnomination''.] '''1.''' The use in juxtaposition of words nearly alike in sound, but of different meanings; a paronomasia.—'''2.''' Alliteration, or the use of two or more words in succession beginning with the same letter or sound. See ''agnomination''.
{{smaller block|Geraldus Cambrensis speaks of ''annomination'', which he describes to be what we call alliteration, as the favourite rhetorical figure both of the Welsh and English in his time.
{{right|''Tyrwhitt'', Chaucer, iii. § 1, note.}}
''Annomination'' plays an important rôle in their sentence-relation [parasynthetic compounds], especially in the first stage of transfer to a simple active signification.
{{right|''Amer. Jour. of Philol.'', II. 198.}}}}

