'''2.''' A thing or circumstance that causes discomfort; an annoyance.
{{smaller block|Good angels guard thee from the boar’s ''annoy.''
{{right|''Shak.'', Rich. III., v. 3.}}}}
{{smaller block|[Now chiefly poetic; the common word in prose is ''annoyance.'']}}

'''annoy''' (a̤-noi′), ''v.'' [Early mod. E. also ''anoy'', ''anoye'', ''anoie'', < ME. ''anoyen'', ''anoien'', ''anuyen'', ''anuien'', ''anyen'', ''anuen'', < OF. ''anoier'', ''enoier'', ''anuier'', ''enuier'', later ''ennuyer'', annoy, vex, weary, irk, mod. F. ''ennuyer'' (see ''ennuyé'') = Pr. ''enoiar'', ''enuiar'' = Sp. Pg. ''enojar'', Pg. also ''anojar'' = It. ''nojare'', ''annojare'', OIt. ''inodiare''; from the noun.] '''I.†''' ''intrans.'' '''1.''' To be hateful or troublesome: followed by ''to''.—'''2.''' [By omission of reflexive pronoun.] To be troubled, disquieted, vexed.
{{smaller block|If that thou ''anoie'' nat or forthenke nat of al thi fortune.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Boëthius, ii. prose 4.}}}}

'''II.''' ''trans.'' To be hateful, troublesome, or vexatious to; trouble, disquiet, disturb, vex, molest, harass, plague; irk, weary, bore, especially by repeated acts: as, to ''annoy'' a person by perpetual questioning; to ''annoy'' the enemy by raids: in the passive, followed by ''at'' or ''about'', formerly by ''of''.
{{smaller block|It bigan to ''anoye'' the puple ''of'' the weie and trauel.
{{right|''Wyclif'', Num. xxi. 4 (Purv.).}}
<poem>Against the Capitol I met a lion,
Who glar’d upon me, and went surly by
Without ''annoying'' me.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', J. C., i. 3.}}
He determined not yet to dismiss them, but merely to humble and ''annoy'' them.
{{right|''Macaulay'', Hist. Eng., iv.}}}}
{{smaller block|='''Syn.''' ''Molest'', ''Plague'', etc. (see ''tease''), trouble, disturb, disquiet, vex, irritate, fret, embarrass, perplex.}}
